{
  "id": "Compound::DB00387 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Procyclidine is likely to interact with Chrm1 through a series of molecular and cellular mechanisms involving G-protein coupled receptors. The evidence suggests that Procyclidine binds to Chrm4, which is a key component of the G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor system. This binding triggers the participation of Chrm4 in activities such as G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity or muscarinic acetylcholine receptor activity, all of which are ultimately linked to Chrm1. The shared participation of Chrm4 in these receptor activities implies a functional relationship between Procyclidine and Chrm1, where the activation of Chrm4 leads to the activation of Chrm1 through downstream signaling pathways. The consistent evidence across the three paths highlights a core mechanism involving the interaction between Procyclidine and Chrm4, which then influences Chrm1 through receptor-mediated signaling.",
